  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a Fourier series?",
      "a": "It writes any periodic signal as a sum of sine and cosine waves at the fundamental frequency and its whole-number multiples, the harmonics, each with its own amplitude and phase.",
      "asks": [
        "What does Fourier series mean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do sine waves make a square wave?",
      "a": "Add the fundamental with amplitude 4 over pi, then the odd harmonics 3, 5, 7 and so on, each with amplitude 4 over n pi. The more terms you add, the flatter the tops and the steeper the edges.",
      "asks": [
        "What harmonics are in a square wave?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a harmonic?",
      "a": "A harmonic is a sine wave at a whole-number multiple of the fundamental frequency. For a 50 hertz signal, the 3rd harmonic is 150 hertz and the 5th is 250 hertz.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the third harmonic?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a square wave have only odd harmonics?",
      "a": "A square wave has half-wave symmetry: its second half is the first half flipped upside down. Even harmonics do not share that symmetry, so their coefficients come out zero.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are there no even harmonics in a square wave?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the Gibbs phenomenon?",
      "a": "When a Fourier series approximates a sudden jump, the sum overshoots by about 9 percent of the jump right beside it. Adding more terms makes the overshoot narrower but never smaller.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the Fourier sum overshoot at the edges?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Joseph Fourier presented trigonometric series for heat flow in 1807 and published them in 1822.",
    "A square wave's Fourier series contains only odd harmonics, with amplitudes 4/(nπ).",
    "The Gibbs overshoot at a jump is about 9% of the jump size."
  ],
